  (a) truncation invariance : rebuild from inputs [0..t*] only, compare the value AT t*
  (b) future perturbation   : corrupt every input after t*, compare all values up to t* (must be bit-identical, 0.000e+00)

  (c) TEETH: 5/5 deliberately non-causal variants were flagged clearly (> 1e-6). The audit has teeth.
        LEAK centred-window            FLAGGED (correct)
        LEAK forward-difference        FLAGGED (correct)
        LEAK full-sample-norm          FLAGGED (correct)
        LEAK reversed-cumulative       FLAGGED (correct)
        LEAK future-peak-drawdown      FLAGGED (correct)

  (c') MEASURED BLIND SPOT — BLINDSPOT scale-inv-reversal
        truncation 1.137e-13, perturbation 4.116e-13: both at float-rounding scale, so NEITHER test sees it.
        Cause: `drawdown = 1 - px/peak` is invariant to a constant multiple of px,
        and truncating a reversed cumsum rescales px by exactly such a constant.
        This is a real limit of the method, stated rather than hidden. It does not
        affect the verdict on dd_accel — the arithmetic identities below show its
        cumulative runs forward — but it does mean these two tests alone could not
        have refuted a scale-invariantly-reversed implementation, so the forward
        direction of `np.cumsum` is checked directly instead.

  (d) LABELS
        observed crisis windows that wrap the grid: 0 (none — label is purely backward)
        y[t] reproduced from breaks at indices <= t only: IDENTICAL (0 cells differ)
        T1 positives: 180 days from 6 onsets (30 per onset, FR32)

  (e) STANDARDISATION
        window_from(as_of=2020-06-15, years=5) -> [15.0, 16.0, 17.0, 18.0, 19.0, 20.0]; admits any value dated after as_of: no
        ecdf_transform(0.5, [0,1]) = 0.5 (ranks against the supplied window only; the window is the caller's, and the only caller is window_from)
        trailing_pct on a strictly INCREASING series: last value's percentile = 1.0000 (must be 1.0000 — a causal window ending at t contains its own maximum at t)
        trailing_pct on a strictly DECREASING series: last value's percentile = 0.0014 (must be the window minimum's rank; a centred or full-sample window would not give this)
        NOT ON THE PATH: t1_auc scores the RAW series. AUC is rank-based (Mann-Whitney U), so no standardisation of any kind enters any AUC reported here. trailing_pct feeds only the alert/precision/recall columns of study_stress_candidates.py, which this report does not use.

  ARITHMETIC IDENTITIES (the definitions, checked against the arrays rather than read)
        dd_accel[t] == drawdown[t] - drawdown[t-5]  : max|delta| = 0.000e+00 over 5,295 cells  (a BACKWARD 5-day difference)
        rv_accel[t] == rv[t] / mean(rv[t-19..t])    : max|delta| = 0.000e+00 over 5,358 cells  (a TRAILING ratio)
        roll(a,w,f)[i] == f(a[i-w+1..i])            : max|delta| = 0.000e+00  (window ENDS at i, never centred on it)
        drawdown uses px = exp(cumsum(lr)) forward  : px[0] == exp(lr[0]) -> 1.0041807746 vs 1.0041807746  (a reversed cumulative would not match)

  >>> H_C1 VERDICT: ACCEPTED — dd_accel and rv_accel are strictly causal

  RESIDUAL CAVEAT, not resolved by the above: `daily_prices.adj_close`
  carries split/dividend adjustment factors that are applied
  retroactively, so the price level at t embeds corporate actions
  announced after t. Log returns are invariant to a constant factor,
  so this perturbs only the handful of days on which an adjustment
  lands; it is a real but small look-ahead that lives in the data,
  not in the feature code, and these tests cannot see it.

